- Some tardigrade species enter a state called cryptobiosis, in which metabolic processes nearly cease, allowing them to endure harsh environments for decades.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Tardigrades, microscopic animals measuring just 0.1 to 1.5 millimeters, can withstand temperatures near absolute zero and pressures exceeding those found in the deepest ocean trenches."},{"key":"B","text":"During the 2007 FOTON-M3 mission, tardigrades became the first animals known to survive direct exposure to the vacuum and radiation of outer space."},{"key":"C","text":"Through a process called cryptobiosis, in which their metabolic activity nearly stops, tardigrades can endure extreme temperatures, crushing pressures, and even the vacuum of outer space for decades."},{"key":"D","text":"Tardigrades are remarkable microscopic animals that have survived conditions ranging from near-absolute-zero temperatures to direct exposure to outer space aboard the FOTON-M3 mission."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to highlight the mechanism behind tardigrades'' resilience. The choice explaining cryptobiosis — the near-cessation of metabolic processes — directly identifies the biological mechanism and connects it to the animals'' ability to endure extreme conditions, fulfilling the stated goal. The choice describing size and survival feats catalogs what tardigrades endure without explaining how. The choice about the FOTON-M3 mission focuses on a single achievement rather than the underlying mechanism. The choice listing a range of survived conditions emphasizes the breadth of resilience but does not address the biological process enabling it.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




- Glassblowing was invented by Syrian artisans around the first century BCE.
